        content: "Behavioural interviews are where Indian candidates most consistently underperform relative to their technical ability.\n\n**Why Indian candidates struggle with behavioural questions:**\n1. Engineering education in India focuses almost entirely on technical skills — soft skills interviews are new\n2. Cultural tendency toward humility makes it uncomfortable to talk about personal achievements\n3. Lack of practice speaking in English under pressure\n4. Generic answers: 'I worked hard and completed the project on time' — no specifics\n\n**The STAR method (must use for every behavioural answer):**\n- **S**ituation: brief context (1–2 sentences)\n- **T**ask: what was your specific responsibility or challenge\n- **A**ction: what YOU did (not 'we' — use 'I')\n- **R**esult: quantifiable outcome where possible\n\n**8 stories every Indian candidate should prepare:**\n1. A project or task that failed — what went wrong and what you learned\n2. A time you had a conflict or disagreement with a teammate or manager — how you resolved it\n3. A time you worked under a very tight deadline — what you did differently\n4. A time you received critical feedback — how you responded and what changed\n5. A time you learned a new technology quickly — the situation and your approach\n6. A time you made a difficult decision with incomplete information\n7. A time you went above your job scope to help the team or project\n8. Your proudest achievement — what made it challenging and what the impact was\n\n**Amazon Leadership Principle tips (if targeting Amazon):**\nAmazon maps every behavioural question to one of 16 Leadership Principles. Research each LP and prepare a specific story for the 8 most commonly assessed: Customer Obsession, Bias for Action, Deliver Results, Ownership, Think Big, Invent and Simplify, Have Backbone, Earn Trust."
