        heading: "Salary Negotiation for Indian Engineers — After the Offer",
        content: "Salary negotiation is culturally unfamiliar for most Indian candidates but can add ₹2–10 LPA with a single 15-minute conversation.\n\n**The negotiation mindset shift:**\nMost Indian engineers accept the first offer without negotiating — seeing the offer as final. Companies in India expect some negotiation at the senior level and are generally fine with it at all levels. Asking doesn't risk your offer being withdrawn.\n\n**What to say when you get an offer:**\n'Thank you for the offer — I'm really excited about the role and the team. I've been doing some research on the market rate for this level, and I was hoping we could discuss the compensation. Based on my experience and the research I've done, I was expecting something in the range of ₹X–Y LPA. Is there flexibility to move closer to that range?'\n\n**What to have ready:**\n- A competing offer (real or stated) — the single most powerful negotiation tool\n- Market rate data: levels.fyi has India compensation data; glassdoor.co.in; LinkedIn Salary; this blog's salary sections\n- Your specific value-add: a specific skill or experience the team particularly needs\n\n**What CAN be negotiated:**\n- Base salary (most important; affects all future raises)\n- Signing/joining bonus (one-time, doesn't affect annual raises)\n- Stock (RSUs): more negotiable at product companies, especially if you have competing offers\n- Work location flexibility (WFH days)\n\n**What usually CANNOT be negotiated:**\n- Job level/band (set by the offer, you'd need to request a re-evaluation which is rare)\n- Benefits package\n- Start date (usually some flexibility)"
